# Standard-chess perft suite — fast tier, runs in CI on every commit.
#
# Format: <FEN> ;D<depth> <nodes> ;D<depth> <nodes> ...
# Blank lines and lines beginning with '#' are ignored.
# One case is one FEN at one depth, so a D4 failure does not hide whether
# D1..D3 were correct.
#
# Every count in this file was generated locally and cross-checked against two
# independent sources: the published Chess Programming Wiki tables, and the
# jchess-perft-dataset. All three agree. Depths are capped so the whole tier
# stays fast enough to run in a debug build; deeper counts live in
# perft-standard-deep.epd.
#
# These six positions are the canonical set. Knowing why each one exists
# matters more than the numbers:
#
#   1. Initial position — the trivial baseline. If this is wrong, nothing else
#      is worth running.
#   2. "Kiwipete" — dense middlegame with castling available to both sides,
#      pins, and many captures. Historically catches more movegen bugs than any
#      other single position.
#   3. Sparse endgame built around en passant and rook/pawn interaction. Narrow
#      enough to reach great depth cheaply, so it is the one to push deep.
#   4. Promotions under pressure, with castling rights on one side only. Exists
#      in two mirrored forms; a side-dependent bug shows up as a disagreement
#      between them.
#   5. Reaches positions where the same square is contested by many pieces at
#      once. Catches bugs in capture generation and in promotion-with-check.
#   6. Quiet-looking, heavily interlocked middlegame. Catches ordering-
#      independent legality bugs that the noisier positions mask.

rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1 ;D1 20 ;D2 400 ;D3 8902 ;D4 197281 ;D5 4865609
r3k2r/p1ppqpb1/bn2pnp1/3PN3/1p2P3/2N2Q1p/PPPBBPPP/R3K2R w KQkq - 0 1 ;D1 48 ;D2 2039 ;D3 97862 ;D4 4085603
8/2p5/3p4/KP5r/1R3p1k/8/4P1P1/8 w - - 0 1 ;D1 14 ;D2 191 ;D3 2812 ;D4 43238 ;D5 674624
r3k2r/Pppp1ppp/1b3nbN/nP6/BBP1P3/q4N2/Pp1P2PP/R2Q1RK1 w kq - 0 1 ;D1 6 ;D2 264 ;D3 9467 ;D4 422333
# Position 4 mirrored: colours and ranks reflected, so the counts must match the
# line above exactly. A disagreement between these two means a bug that treats
# one colour differently — pawn direction, promotion rank, or castling side.
r2q1rk1/pP1p2pp/Q4n2/bbp1p3/Np6/1B3NBn/pPPP1PPP/R3K2R b KQ - 0 1 ;D1 6 ;D2 264 ;D3 9467 ;D4 422333
